["<aside class=\"callout why\" role=\"note\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Elk give you two short windows a day. Be in the right seat when they open, and do not burn the day walking through their beds.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Twenty four hour clock of an elk day in late September. Feeding peaks start after dawn, about 6:30 to 10, and again at dusk, about 17:30 to 20:30. Midday bedded in timber. At night feeding continues and rutting bulls move. An Alberta GPS collar study found peaks at dawn and dusk, with the morning peak almost 2 hours after first light. Times are a sketch, Tip.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">An elk day (late September)</text><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"165\" r=\"112\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">00</text><text x=\"201\" y=\"99\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">03</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">06</text><text x=\"201\" y=\"241\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">09</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">12</text><text x=\"59\" y=\"241\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">15</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">18</text><text x=\"59\" y=\"99\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">21</text><path d=\"M77.8 151.0 A54 54 0 0 1 183.5 172.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3d4a6b\" stroke-width=\"14\" stroke-linecap=\"butt\"/><path d=\"M203.4 174.7 A74 74 0 0 1 167.0 229.1\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"18\" stroke-linecap=\"butt\"/><path d=\"M56.6 174.7 A74 74 0 0 1 71.3 120.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"18\" stroke-linecap=\"butt\"/><path d=\"M149.2 236.5 A74 74 0 0 1 61.6 193.3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"18\" stroke-linecap=\"butt\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"165\" r=\"36\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">24 h</text><rect x=\"258\" y=\"70\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"282\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Feed</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">after dawn, dusk</text><rect x=\"258\" y=\"104\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/><text x=\"282\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Bed in timber</text><rect x=\"258\" y=\"138\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#3d4a6b\"/><text x=\"282\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Night</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">feed, rut moves</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Alberta collars:</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">dawn, dusk peaks (80%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Two peaks: after dawn and at dusk <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Midday: bedded near cover <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what this chapter covers</summary><ul><li>Bed, feed, water; the midday bed; weather (wind, rain, snow, cold fronts, barometer); the moon; hunting pressure; and how strong the evidence is for each.</li><li>An Alberta GPS (Global Positioning System) collar study: elk were crepuscular, with activity peaks after sunrise and at the end of evening twilight; the morning peak came almost 2 hours after first light; Canadian elk were mostly active by day; more active in summer; weakest pattern in winter (80%, Ensing and others 2014).</li><li>The authors put the daily pattern down mostly to human disturbance <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Times on the clock are a sketch for late September (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cross section of an elk day. Bed in timber on a north slope. At dusk the herd walks out to feed in a meadow, burn or cutblock, with water or a wallow nearby. In the morning they drift back into the timber. A BC habitat model notes most elk stay within 183 m of cover.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Bed, feed, water</text><path d=\"M0 230 L120 120 L180 150 L400 150 L400 300 L0 300 Z\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g transform=\"translate(30 205) scale(0.8)\"><rect x=\"-3\" y=\"-10\" width=\"6\" height=\"12\" rx=\"1\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><path d=\"M0 -50 L16 -10 L-16 -10 Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(55 185) scale(0.8)\"><rect x=\"-3\" y=\"-10\" width=\"6\" height=\"12\" rx=\"1\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><path d=\"M0 -50 L16 -10 L-16 -10 Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(80 160) scale(0.8)\"><rect x=\"-3\" y=\"-10\" width=\"6\" height=\"12\" rx=\"1\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><path d=\"M0 -50 L16 -10 L-16 -10 Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(105 140) scale(0.8)\"><rect x=\"-3\" y=\"-10\" width=\"6\" height=\"12\" rx=\"1\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><path 